          "id": "P1",
          "name": "Patches never built by the author or by CI (H1)",
          "description": "Patches are routinely posted, and sometimes merged, without the touched objects being compiled by the author, the bots or the integrator. Standard configs (defconfig, one randconfig, syzbot's fixed set) cover a minority of patched lines; allyesconfig covers most but is slow and still misses code. Torvalds and Rothwell describe code reaching linux-next never compiled, and Torvalds at the Dec 2025 Maintainers Summit said basic configs go untested. The measurements are at line level (they include #ifdef arms) rather than object level. The Gazzillo/Yildiran/Oh group authored most of the numbers. No public data says how often 0-day never builds a patched file.",
          "who_has_it": "Patch authors, subsystem maintainers, linux-next integrators, CI operators",
