          "existing_solutions": "0-day/kernel test robot, linux-next daily builds, KernelCI, TuxSuite, syzbot patch testing; krepair/klocalizer for repair; allmodconfig on x86_64 as the Torvalds proxy.",
          "remaining_gap": "No pre-posting, per-object answer to 'is each touched object built by the configs I (or CI) used'. There is no per-object coverage report for bots. Header-only patches (about 2%) and #ifdef arms are not covered at object level.",
          "kfold_fit": "partial",
          "kfold_fit_reason": "The object-level question (is the touched .o built under config X, and under which configs) is exactly kfold's output. The headline statistics are line-level and include #ifdef and header cases that kfold cannot see, so object coverage will be higher than the quoted line coverage. Do not reuse the numbers as kfold's gap without re-measuring at object level."
        },
        {
          "id": "P2",
          "name": "Find a .config that builds a given file or patch, with minimal change to a base config (H2)",
          "description": "To test a patch, a developer must work out which CONFIG options (and which architecture) make the touched files build. This means tracing select/depends across Kconfig and Makefile conditions. The krepair paper's motivating example is ARM_GIC, which is reachable only via ARM_GIC_PM, which depends on PM. Forum users and Yocto integrators ask the same thing from the object side. The kernel docs answer it by 'read the Makefile' (277) or 'search menuconfig' (140). Research tools (klocalizer/krepair) solve it at line level with SuperC and report 98.5% coverage while changing under 1.53% of the base config. A KUnit author must hand-write .kunitconfig dependencies.",
          "who_has_it": "Patch authors, maintainers, reviewers, KUnit/test authors, embedded integrators, kernel researchers",
