      "coverage_gaps": "\"1) lore.kernel.org was blocked (403) and WebSearch budget ran out, so there is no full-text evidence on whether reviewers ask 'did you compile test with config X' or whether developers ask 'which CONFIG builds X'; claim 17 is absence of evidence only. 2) No maintainer survey or interview data and no developer-survey results on build/config pain; the only survey data are KernelCI 2020 (282) and a ConfigFix-cited survey (90). 3) No frequency data for Makefile-level bugs on a recent kernel (P6 relies on anecdotes), and no data on how often stable breakage is caused by object selection rather than code or toolchain. 4) Almost all evidence concerns Linux; BusyBox, Barebox, U-Boot and coreboot communities (the other evaluated projects) have no claims, nor does Rust-for-Linux Kbuild coverage or device-tree objects beyond docs. 5) LPC 2026 Build Systems/Testing MCs, Kernel Recipes 2026, and kernelnewbies were not reachable or not covered. 6) The derivation method of CIP kernel-cve-triage's file-compiled check and of cvehound was not verified (160 is a snippet), yet these are the closest P10 competitors; PatchLens details also differ across sources (123). 7) No evidence of what Kconfig/arch handling kfold would need for 11% cross-compile patches, and Android/GKI and distro evidence is largely documentation and old threads (2011-2018). 8) Several claims came through fetch summarizers (57, 63, 73, 103, 157, 169, 196), and unverifiable pages (HAL, Gentoo forum, login-walled) weaken the statistics. 9) Duplicate or conflicting claims need reconciling before citing: 20 (about 2,000 CVEs/month) vs 154/156 (about 60 per week); 158 vs 204 (Ericsson/Yocto 70%, possibly the same talk); 70 vs 210; 53/77/111/172/173/174/267-270 are all the same krepair paper, and the group of 48-52/113 is the same group's slides, so count as one source for independence. 10) Claim 129's 'fixed small set of config files' and 113's 42% syzbot figure are from slides, not the paper.\""
    }
